Lime stone \(\left(\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}\right)\) used in the extraction of Fe from haematite in blast furnace get decomposed to \(\mathrm{CaO}\), which removes silicate impurity (gangue) of the ore as molten slag.
$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{CaCO}_{3} \xrightarrow{\Delta} \mathrm{CaO}+\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\
& \mathrm{CaO}+\mathrm{SiO}_{2} \xrightarrow{\Delta}  \mathrm{CaSiO}_{3}
\end{aligned}\)$

Red bauxite which contains $\mathrm{Fe}_2 \mathrm{O}_3$ as the main impurity, is refined either by Baeyer's process or by Hall's process. White bauxite containing $\mathrm{SiO}_2$ impurity is refined by Serpeck's method. In Serpeck's method, following reactions take place.
$\begin{gathered}
\mathrm{Al}_2 \mathrm{O}_3 \cdot 2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}+3 \mathrm{C}+\mathrm{N}_2 \stackrel{1800^{{}^{\circ}} \mathrm{C}}{\longrightarrow} 2 \mathrm{AlN}+3 \mathrm{CO}+2 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \\
\mathrm{AlN}+3 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O} \longrightarrow \mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{OH})_3+\mathrm{NH}_3 \\
2 \mathrm{Al}(\mathrm{OH})_3 \longrightarrow \mathrm{Al}_2 \mathrm{O}_3+3 \mathrm{H}_2 \mathrm{O}
\end{gathered}$
